### 🔹 Time Complexity

* Measures **how runtime grows** with input size (n)
* Counts **operations**, not seconds

### 🔹 Space Complexity

* Measures **extra memory used** by an algorithm

---

## 📊 Big-O Notation (Growth Rate)

| Big-O      | Name        | Typical Example      |
| ---------- | ----------- | -------------------- |
| O(1)       | Constant    | Access array element |
| O(log n)   | Logarithmic | Binary Search        |
| O(n)       | Linear      | Single loop          |
| O(n log n) | Linear-Log  | Merge Sort           |
| O(n²)      | Quadratic   | Nested loops         |
| O(2ⁿ)      | Exponential | Recursive Fibonacci  |

---

## 🔁 Best, Worst & Average Case

| Case    | Meaning       | Example (Linear Search)         |
| ------- | ------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Best    | Minimum time  | Element at first index → O(1)   |
| Average | Expected time | Element in middle → O(n)        |
| Worst   | Maximum time  | Element last / not found → O(n) |

---

## 🧠 Full Explanation (Exam + Interview Ready)

### 1️⃣ Time Complexity

Shows how execution time **increases with input size**.

Example:

```python
for i in range(n):
    print(i)
```

➡️ Runs n times → **O(n)**

---

### 2️⃣ Space Complexity

Extra memory required excluding input.

```python
def func(n):
    x = 10
```

➡️ **O(1)** (constant space)

```python
def func(n):
    arr = [0] * n
```

➡️ **O(n)** (linear space)

---

### 3️⃣ Why Big-O ignores constants

```python
for i in range(n):
    print(i)
for i in range(n):
    print(i)
```

Operations = 2n → **O(n)**

👉 Constants don’t matter for large input sizes

---

### 4️⃣ Time vs Space Trade-Off

* More memory → faster execution
* Less memory → slower execution

Choose based on **constraints**

---
* Time Complexity → Speed
* Space Complexity → Memory
* Big-O → Growth rate
* Best / Worst / Avg → Performance cases

